adio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf27b9b5-6f76-5f6f-9c9b-5a9cc802a78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385620} {:answer/id #uuid "e441a379-d79b-55dc-aced-c064ece2c4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385621} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cb090c-b834-52e8-8b20-be52ed47518a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385622} {:answer/id #uuid "c62ebeda-0907-50be-a525-02d30793a0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385623} {:answer/id #uuid "47b1eae5-06e5-5fcf-99f0-8f6ca3dc9f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0cb090c-b834-52e8-8b20-be52ed47518a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385622} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e3da7b-87ec-5072-846e-3d386080bae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f83b131-cba7-5925-800d-f4bed8f0ae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-76250" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}-2$\n- $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $g$ has two roots, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ So, we have $g(-\\sqrt{2})=0=g(\\sqrt{2}),$ while $-\\sqrt{2}\\ne \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c55b73a7-434a-561a-a9bf-2852a6f64c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150930} {:answer/id #uuid "d72387c9-c0a0-5572-b093-ffd4df7bb342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150931} {:answer/id #uuid "f5673a2b-d0a9-52b4-8cf0-cd33e664afa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150932} {:answer/id #uuid "480d13fe-31f3-5cbb-