 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16a1e15e-b250-589e-b4bc-6048810dc9dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-76259" :question/problem "![](images/06/06e3c48955dc8f907c87a93aeaa7c2a7240ea194fe1ca6a96b2a0bf566882264.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)=0.25{x}^{2}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](images/fc/fce55ada0375bff9bd3ea5852e80f707d70f145211b60e80b5a16b3049020000.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e16a0a6d-81a1-57e4-9dde-ff184919f4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150936} {:answer/id #uu