adio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a881f7f-2aac-56cf-b1e8-aaedca1189bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382298} {:answer/id #uuid "4f982ebf-cff3-5505-8a98-581cac40b008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382299} {:answer/id #uuid "778461b2-98fd-56db-b0f8-ab5dca8c4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382300} {:answer/id #uuid "f91c2f6b-86bf-5f7d-8dff-c8efc483f589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382301} {:answer/id #uuid "f38fb309-7eb7-55d6-a920-f933e6d3abfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91c2f6b-86bf-5f7d-8dff-c8efc483f589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382301} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4fcb32-650f-532a-93a9-8335d685209a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4cd6be0-845e-5684-aa03-e5a2472b9ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-77378" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- $g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the range. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051035 :topic/math-academy-id 2678 :topic/title "Injections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44dfbe1-7b7f-504f-8ef4-18d92ab5c33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151128} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e059d7-b8aa-56e4-87d7-e872e7488b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151129} {:answer/id #uuid "0a0a263e-6114-5dce-9b7f-f2cdbf11031e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151130} {:answer/id #uuid "25ca756e-fba1-5d9d-a0c9-c43e696e8cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151131} {:answer/id #uuid "5c