:answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385763} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b620ff-b07a-539e-848b-5a4495a02b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385764} {:answer/id #uuid "3d20a11b-a7f0-5f86-a73e-b42148ebad7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385765} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa6b498-2dd2-5cb7-8ced-5dc9ba664481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385766} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbfccba-c8ae-5eaa-971e-31abfa9ab07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b620ff-b07a-539e-848b-5a4495a02b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385764} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bf18a4-4f80-5aef-aed9-a5c5b7c38441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b7fec18-8819-50a6-ad9e-b4237d8c4fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82109" :question/problem "![](images/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7aa7daf-32d2-5eba-98ef-47b99828b454" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When Fermat's Little Theorem Is Applicable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049743 :topic/math-academy-id 2726 :topic/title "Fermat's Little Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a087d9dc-290b-591e-8161-361b42d40a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151572} {:answer/id #uuid "034d9ab6-8a15-5b2b-8d49-025c609b7162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151573} {:answer/id #uuid "0840a78e-b809-5ff6-8cfd-0391871dcdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151574} {:answer/id #uuid "22924f1f-b7c9-5270-ad2f-3baf966b89f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151575} {:answer/id #uuid